@* Extensions (UI docs): building an extension's screens with the library - imports, assets, isolation, Level 3 CSS,
   overlays, dnfUI.init and an extension's own Tailwind build. *@

<p>Extensions use the same library as the CMS, so an admin tab or a dashboard widget looks like the admin around it, in
    light and dark. This page covers the library side; the manifest, slots, views and resources of an extension are in
    <code>.docs/guides/extension-development.md</code>. The sample <code>extensions/admin/audit-dashboard</code> follows
    everything below.</p>

<h2 id="assembly">The assembly</h2>
<ul>
    <li><code>DotNetForge.Sdk</code> references <code>DotNetForge.UI</code>, so an extension project that compiles against
        the SDK can use the Dnf types (<code>DnfSize</code>, <code>DnfTone</code>, <code>DnfAssets</code>,
        <code>DnfTimestampTagHelper.Relative</code> …).</li>
    <li>The web host ships <code>DotNetForge.UI.dll</code>, and the extension loader resolves every assembly the host
        ships from the host - there is one copy of the tag helper types at runtime.</li>
    <li>Extension views are compiled at runtime against the host's references, so they can register the tag helpers -
        a views-only extension needs no project at all.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="admin-tab">An admin tab</h2>
<p>An admin tab (<code>type: admin</code>) is a complete HTML document in an iframe. It inherits nothing from the admin,
    so it loads the library itself. Register the tag helpers in <code>Views/_ViewImports.cshtml</code>:</p>

<ui-doc-code id="ext-imports" lang="razor">
@@using DotNetForge.UI
@@using DotNetForge.UI.Components
@@addTagHelper *, DotNetForge.UI
</ui-doc-code>

<p>and load the assets in <code>Views/Shared/_Layout.cshtml</code> (set by <code>Views/_ViewStart.cshtml</code>):</p>

<ui-doc-code id="ext-layout" lang="razor">
@@{ var resourceBase = ViewData["ResourceBase"] as string ?? string.Empty; }<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta name="color-scheme" content="light dark" />
    <title>@@ViewData["Title"]</title>
    <script src="/_content/DotNetForge.UI/dnf-theme.js"></script>          @* first: theme before the first paint *@
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/_content/DotNetForge.UI/dnf.css" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@@resourceBase/css/index.css" />           @* yours, after dnf.css *@
    <script src="/_content/DotNetForge.UI/dnf.js" defer></script>           @* only if you use its hooks *@
</head>
<body class="dnf-scope" data-dnf-extension="yourcompany.admin.reports">
    @@RenderBody()
    <script src="@@resourceBase/js/index.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
</ui-doc-code>

<ul>
    <li><code>dnf-theme.js</code> reads the same <code>localStorage</code> key (<code>dnf-theme</code>) as the admin, so the
        tab follows the user's light or dark choice (<a href="@UiDocsCatalog.Root/theming#switching">Theming</a>).</li>
    <li><code>dnf.js</code> is optional: load it when your views use <code>data-*</code> hooks - tooltips, dropdowns, tabs,
        collections, confirm (<a href="@UiDocsCatalog.Root/behaviours">Behaviours</a>). Toasts and the confirmation
        dialog also need their markup in your layout (<a href="@UiDocsCatalog.Root/behaviours#toasts">toasts</a>,
        <a href="@UiDocsCatalog.Root/behaviours#confirm">confirmation dialog</a>); without the dialog,
        <code>data-confirm</code> falls back to the browser's <code>confirm()</code>. The audit dashboard needs neither and
        leaves <code>dnf.js</code> out.</li>
    <li>The paths are the <code>DnfAssets</code> constants (<code>@@DnfAssets.Stylesheet</code> …) if you prefer them; they
        include no app path base.</li>
    <li>No inline <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> or <code>style</code>: the admin's Content Security Policy applies to the tab
        too. Scripts and stylesheets live in <code>Views/Resources</code>.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="widget">A dashboard widget</h2>
<p>A widget (<code>Views/Widget.cshtml</code>) is a partial rendered inside a card on the admin dashboard, which already
    loads the library: use the classes and utilities directly, and add the <code>DotNetForge.UI</code> lines to its
    <code>_ViewImports.cshtml</code> only when it uses tag helpers. The sample <code>extensions/widgets/weather-widget</code>
    uses Tailwind utilities and no stylesheet. Page modules and themes render on the public site, which has its own
    styles.</p>

<h2 id="isolation">Isolation</h2>
<p>Mark the extension's root element - the <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> of an admin tab - with two attributes:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong><code>class="dnf-scope"</code></strong> derives the hover and soft shades again on that element, so
        overriding a leaf token there re-themes everything inside, shades included
        (<a href="@UiDocsCatalog.Root/theming#derived">derived tokens</a>).</li>
    <li><strong><code>data-dnf-extension="&lt;id&gt;"</code></strong> names the extension - its manifest <code>id</code>.
        The library attaches no behaviour to it: it is the hook for your own selectors, and it makes your markup easy to
        find in the DOM.</li>
    <li>Prefix your own classes with something of your own (<code>reports-…</code>), never <code>dnf-</code>.</li>
</ul>

<ui-doc-code id="ext-scope-css" lang="css">
/* Views/Resources/css/index.css - unlayered */
[data-dnf-extension="yourcompany.admin.reports"] {
    --dnf-primary: #2563eb;
    --dnf-primary-foreground: #ffffff;
    --dnf-primary-text: light-dark(#1d4ed8, #93c5fd);
}
</ui-doc-code>

<ui-doc-example id="isolation-demo" title="An extension root" description="The same markup twice: as the admin draws it, and inside an extension root whose stylesheet overrides the primary colour. Buttons, badges, soft tints and links follow." layout="block">
    <div class="dnf-grid dnf-grid--cols-2">
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-surface p-5">
            <div class="dnf-stack">
                <div class="dnf-spread">
                    <p class="font-semibold">The admin</p>
                    <dnf-badge tone="Primary">Beta</dnf-badge>
