<h2 id="overriding">Overriding components</h2>
<p>Write your stylesheet <strong>without</strong> <code>@@layer</code>. Unlayered CSS beats every layer of
    <code>dnf.css</code> - <code>theme</code>, <code>base</code>, <code>components</code>, <code>utilities</code> - with
    ordinary selectors and no <code>!important</code> (<a href="@UiDocsCatalog.Root/customization#level-3">Level 3</a>):</p>

<ui-doc-code id="ext-override-css" lang="css">
/* Views/Resources/css/index.css - unlayered */
.reports-summary .dnf-card__body { padding: var(--dnf-space-4); }
.reports-toolbar .dnf-button { --dnf-button-height: var(--dnf-control-sm); }
</ui-doc-code>

<p>Use tokens (<code>var(--dnf-…)</code>) rather than literal values, so light and dark keep working. Don't restyle a
    component's internals beyond what you need: its markup and token names are the API; anything else may change.</p>

<h2 id="overlays">Overlays</h2>
<p>Dropdown menus, popovers, dialogs and the tooltip open in the browser's <strong>top layer</strong> (the Popover API and
    <code>&lt;dialog&gt;.showModal()</code>). They are drawn above everything regardless of <code>z-index</code>,
    <code>overflow: hidden</code> or stacking contexts, so an extension never needs a <code>z-index</code> to show them.
    Everything in an admin tab stays inside its iframe.</p>

<ui-doc-example id="overlay-demo" title="A menu inside a clipping box" description="The box hides its overflow; the menu opens in the top layer and shows in full.">
    <div class="h-24 w-80 overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-border bg-surface p-3 text-sm">
        <div class="dnf-spread">
            <span class="font-semibold">report.pdf</span>
            <dnf-dropdown label="Actions for report.pdf">
                <dnf-dropdown-button icon="pencil" data-docs-toast="Rename - a demo, nothing changed.">Rename</dnf-dropdown-button>
                <dnf-dropdown-button icon="download" data-docs-toast="Download - a demo, nothing was downloaded.">Download</dnf-dropdown-button>
                <dnf-dropdown-separator />
                <dnf-dropdown-button icon="trash" danger="true" data-docs-confirm="report.pdf is removed from the media library." data-docs-confirm-title="Delete file" data-docs-confirm-action="Delete">Delete</dnf-dropdown-button>
            </dnf-dropdown>
        </div>
        <p class="text-muted">Uploaded 3 Oct 2026 - 482 KB</p>
    </div>
</ui-doc-example>

<h2 id="added-later">Markup added later</h2>
<p><code>dnf.js</code> wires its per-element behaviours (tabs, collections, view switches, file uploads, frames,
    <code>data-js-only</code>, check groups) once, at load. After inserting markup - a fetched fragment, a cloned template
    - call <code>init</code> on the container. The delegated behaviours (dropdowns, tooltips, dialogs, confirm, copy …)
    need no call.</p>

<ui-doc-code id="ext-init-js" lang="js">
const response = await fetch(url);
panel.innerHTML = await response.text();
window.dnfUI.init(panel);
</ui-doc-code>

<h2 id="tailwind">Tailwind in an extension</h2>
<p>The host's <code>dnf.css</code> contains <strong>only the utilities used by the CMS and the extensions in this
    repository</strong>: Tailwind generated them from the files it scanned at build time
    (<a href="@UiDocsCatalog.Root/customization#build">the build</a>). A bundled extension, under <code>extensions/</code>,
    gets its utilities into <code>dnf.css</code> on the next build. An extension built elsewhere may use a utility the
    host never compiled - and then it has no effect. Such an extension either sticks to the component classes, the
    tokens and its own CSS, or compiles its own utilities with the Tailwind v4 CLI.</p>
<p>Its own build imports Tailwind's <strong>theme and utilities only</strong> - not preflight, which the host's
    <code>dnf.css</code> already applies - plus the DotNetForge theme mapping, so <code>text-muted</code>,
    <code>bg-surface</code>, <code>text-sm</code> and <code>dark:</code> mean the same as in the host. For example a
    <code>reports.css</code> in the extension's <code>Styles</code> folder:</p>

<ui-doc-code id="ext-tailwind-css" lang="css">
@@layer theme, base, components, utilities;
@@import "tailwindcss/theme.css" layer(theme);
@@import "tailwindcss/utilities.css" layer(utilities) source(none);
@@import "../../../../src/DotNetForge.UI/Styles/theme.css";   /* or a copy of it */
@@source "../Views";
</ui-doc-code>

<ui-doc-code id="ext-tailwind-command" lang="text">
tailwindcss --input Styles/reports.css --output Views/Resources/css/reports.css --minify
</ui-doc-code>

<ul>
    <li><code>source(none)</code> turns off automatic detection; <code>@@source</code> names the folders to scan, relative
        to the stylesheet.</li>
    <li>Link the output <strong>after</strong> <code>dnf.css</code>. Its rules are in the same named layers as the host's,
        so they merge into them: your utilities sit in <code>utilities</code>, above the components, like the host's.</li>
    <li>Use the host's Tailwind version, <code>4.3.3</code>; in a checkout the binary is already in
        <code>tools/tailwind/4.3.3/</code>.</li>
    <li>The host's build does not run this step: run it yourself (or in your CI) and ship the generated file in
        <code>Views/Resources/</code>, which is read at request time and must not be written at runtime.</li>
    <li><code>Styles/theme.css</code> only maps names to <code>--dnf-*</code> tokens; the values come from the host's
        <code>dnf.css</code> at runtime.</li>
</ul>
<p>No extension in this repository does this today: the bundled ones are scanned by the host's build.</p>

<h2 id="checklist">Checklist</h2>
<ul>
    <li><code>_ViewImports.cshtml</code> has the three <code>DotNetForge.UI</code> lines.</li>
    <li>Admin tab: <code>dnf-theme.js</code> in <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>, then <code>dnf.css</code>, then your CSS;
        <code>dnf.js</code> if you use hooks.</li>
    <li>The root element has <code>class="dnf-scope" data-dnf-extension="&lt;id&gt;"</code>.</li>
    <li>Your CSS is unlayered, uses tokens and has no <code>!important</code>; no inline <code>style</code> or
        <code>&lt;script&gt;</code>.</li>
    <li><code>dnfUI.init(root)</code> after inserting markup that needs per-element behaviours.</li>
    <li>Every utility you use is in the host's <code>dnf.css</code> or in your own compiled CSS.</li>
</ul>
